{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/676171-homebrewed-uratha-creation-myths","id":676171,"title":"Homebrewed Uratha Creation Myths","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-09-24T01:34:15-07:00","id":676171,"title":"Homebrewed Uratha Creation Myths","content":"In my game I like to play with the idea that different werewolf cultures across the world have different traditions and stories for how the People came to be. In second edition for example we learn that some rat hosts believe the Uratha are &quot;wolf hosts&quot; and that each werewolf is a shard of the original <i>Urfarah</i>. That has interesting connotations when mixed with Ivory Talon lore which potentially involves cannibalism as a way to &quot;reclaim&quot; werewolves who didn't meet their exacting standards of purity. I'd love to know what other stories people have used in their games as alternative lore for the Forsaken.<br /> <br /> It's a relatively minor change, but in my game some Forsaken tell a version of the story where human tribesmen slew <i>Urfarah</i> with the Uratha's blessing and it was only afterwards that the People took human shape so as to walk among the greatest hunters in Pangea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1318,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","user":"kindledRadial","date":". 09-24-2015, 04:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-24T06:23:18-07:00","id":676341,"title":"","content"